<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gemistos Plethon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1355–1454</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as George Gemistos Plethon, Plethon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Gemistos Plethon was a Byzantine philosopher who taught at Mistra in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>. A follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, he proposed radical reforms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>'s government, army and land, and wrote a Book of Laws describing a revived pagan religion, which was later burned. His lectures at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> in 1438–1439 inspired Italian interest in Plato.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon.jpg" alt="Gemistos Plethon" width="248" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benozzo Gozzoli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benozzo_Gozzoli,_Pletone,_Cappella_dei_Magi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
